        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "45f9a0fb5d24815c301fbb27af0d9516201932b3",
            "parents": [
                "055f828af78e6f21efb2019108fa385ecce167c4"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/58/87958/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Jason Hsu",
                "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
            },
            "createdOn": 1772506044,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jason Hsu",
                "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't understand the difference between this factory reset and the initial factory reset.  How are we able to get this file generated sometimes but not others?  Is this a bug in phosphor-networkd that we're just hacking around?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "The file /etc/systemd/network/00-bmc-eth0.network is not always generated because systemd-networkd can fall back to the default configuration located in /usr/lib/systemd/network/.\nOur platform requires the MAC address to be explicitly configured using the FRU provided value. When the file is missing, the interface may fall back to the default configuration without the expected MAC setting.\nThe network-init-once service simply check if the file exists and recreates it only when it is missing. It does not modify existing configurations. This is intended to restore the platform specific configuration rather than working around a phosphor-networkd bug."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Part of my contention was the way this was documented here in the commit message.  I think that an initial boot of a fresh SPI also has this potential problem, not just factory resets, right?\n\nHow does Bletchley and Ventura2 handle this case today?  Whatever we're doing probably needs to be handled for those systems also?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "A fresh SPI will go through the AC power on sequence upon first boot, which trigge systemd to generate the 00-bmc-eth0.network file. This behavior is the same as our AC cycle, so the network file will exist and the issue observed after Redfish ResetAll (direct reboot) does not occur on a fresh SPI.\n\nFor v2, a Redfish ResetAll can also cause the network file to be missing, but the same approach we implemented for Ventura v1 ensuring the 00-bmc-eth0.network file exists and populating the MAC from EntityManager can be applied to handle these platforms as well. We will notify the v2 team so they can implement a similar change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> We will notify the v2 team so they can implement a similar change.\n\nI don't want a \"similar change\".  I want one change that applies for all non-NCSI devices.  We have Ventura, Bletchley, Bletchley1.5, Ventura2, and SanMiguel that all use a non-NCSI network connection.  I don't want 5+ copies of similar code."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> A fresh SPI will go through the AC power on sequence upon first boot, which trigge systemd to generate the 00-bmc-eth0.network file. This behavior is the same as our AC cycle, so the network file will exist and the issue observed after Redfish ResetAll (direct reboot) does not occur on a fresh SPI.\n\nWhy is an AC cycle different though?  I don't understand the why.  AC cycle and ResetAll both do a BMC reboot, both of them should end up with a freshly formatted ubifs volume, why do they behave different?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "We appreciate your suggestion. We will discuss with the Ventura2 team how to implement a solution that can cover all non-NCSI network platforms.\n\nThe key difference is that the network configuration file is only\ngenerated during an AC cycle. A normal reboot does not trigger the\nsame behavior, so the 00-bmc-eth0.network file is not created and\nthe issue occurs.\n\nIn our original flow, using \"fw_setenv openbmconce factory-reset\"\nrequire an AC cycle afterward to work correctly. If only a reboot\nis performed, the system will hit the same issue.\n\nSince Redfish ResetAll performs a factory reset followed by an\nimmediate reboot (not an AC cycle), it also exposes this problem.\n\nThe purpose of this service is to fix this issue by ensuring the\nrequired network configuration file is present even in reboot only\nscenarios such as Redfish ResetAll."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> The key difference is that the network configuration file is only\n> generated during an AC cycle. A normal reboot does not trigger the\n> same behavior, so the 00-bmc-eth0.network file is not created and\n> the issue occurs.\n\nCan you elaborate on what is causing the difference here?  I still don't understand why it would be different."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "From the journal comparison, the difference is caused by the state when\nxyz.openbmc_project.Network.service run.\n\nDuring an AC cycle, the required inventory (MAC address from EntityManager) is already available when the service start, so phosphor-network-manager is able to generate the network configuration:\n\"Wrote networkd file: /etc/systemd/network/00-bmc-eth0.network\".\n\nHowever, after a factory reset followed by a reboot, the service start\nbefore the inventory is fully ready. As a result, it fails to obtain the\nMAC address:\n\"No Object has implemented the interface\n xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.NetworkInterface\"\nand therefore skip generating the network file.\n\nSo the difference is not AC or reboot itself, but whether the required\ninventory data is ready at the time the network service run."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> However, after a factory reset followed by a reboot, the service start\n> before the inventory is fully ready. As a result, it fails to obtain the\n> MAC address:\n> \"No Object has implemented the interface\n> xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.NetworkInterface\"\n> and therefore skip generating the network file.\n\nThis is clearly a bug.  Why aren't we fixing it?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "You are right and we appreciate your feedback. We will take this direction and work on fixing it properly."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "We have updated the patch to address the issue you pointed out regarding the missing configuration file after a factory reset and reboot.\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-networkd/+/88693\nWe would appreciate it if you could take another look when you have a chance. Thank you."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why did we decide to put this with systemd instead of phosphor-networkd?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "The main reason we implemented this as a systemd service is to keep the change platform specific without modifying the upstream phosphor-networkd behavior.\nThe issue only affects the Ventura platform where the expected /etc/systemd/network/00-bmc-eth0.network file may not exist after certain reset flow. Rather than introducing platform specific logic into phosphor-networkd, we chose ventura-network-init service that verifies the configuration and restores it only if it is missing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we make this handle all fb-fixed-nic devices (Ventura2 has 2 MACs)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, this would allow the same logic to support the platform like Ventura v2 that use multiple fixed MAC address. If the solution for v1 is accepted, we will notify the v2 team so they can implement a similar change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "This code got put into meta-facebook root but has Ventura-specific content here.  This is inaccurate.\n\n1. I think we need to key off the `fb-fixed-nic` feature?\n2. We shouldn't be reading from FruDevice, but using the Item.NetworkInterface from EntityManager so that we do not need to know which EEPROM field contains the MAC.\n3. You should probably use the config.json file that is installed with phosphor-network?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "1. Yes, the logic is specific to the Ventura platform. To keep platform specific behavior properly scoped, we have moved the implementation into the Ventura layer (meta-ventura) so it only applies to Ventura systems and does not affect other Facebook platforms.\n\n2. Agreed. The updated implementation retrieves the MAC address from the EntityManager interface xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.NetworkInterface.\n\n3. That is a good suggestion. Instead of duplicating the full configuration, we leverage the existing default from phosphor-network and only apply the minimal Ventura specific override required (MAC address and eth0 match)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 39,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "How do we get this so that we're not creating an entirely new config file here?  phosphor-networkd already attempts to create this file,correct?  It seems like we should be able to leverage 95% of what they're doing and just fix up the right portions."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 39,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "The default config is generic and lack interface match and MAC assignment, so we generate a minimal eth0 specific config while reusing existing default."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 70,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I suspect these are the only two parts you actually need written here, right?  Can't we use a new config snippet file for that?  See for instance the various `.link` and `.network` files that have `[Match]` in meta-facebook; many of them have far less content than this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/recipes-core/systemd/systemd/network-init-once.sh",
                    "line": 70,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, the configuration has been reduced to the minimal required sections.\n\nA snippet only approach (MAC) would be ideal, but since the base file (/etc/systemd/network/00-bmc-eth0.network) may be missing after certain reset flow, we still need to generate a complete interface configuration to ensure consistent behavior."
                }
            ],
